Yes — there are two result-affecting bugs:

1. `data[i] == max_val` compares the current element to the best value seen so far, not to the value of the current streak. When the streak changes, the code never starts counting the new value, so the run of `1`s is not accumulated.
2. The loop never checks the final streak after the loop ends. If the longest streak is at the end of the vector, it is ignored unless a later element ends it.

This version keeps the original non-empty input assumption.

<solution language="rust" verdict="fix">
fn longest_streak(data: &Vec<i32>) -> (i32, usize) {
    let mut max_val = data[0];
    let mut max_count: usize = 1;
    let mut current_count: usize = 1;

    for i in 1..data.len() {
        if data[i] == data[i - 1] {
            current_count += 1;
        } else {
            if current_count > max_count {
                max_count = current_count;
                max_val = data[i - 1];
            }
            current_count = 1;
        }
    }

    if current_count > max_count {
        max_count = current_count;
        max_val = data[data.len() - 1];
    }

    (max_val, max_count)
}
</solution>

